# Test: every light in the room, one at a time
# Started by hand from the control page. It walks every addressable light on every body -
# twenty of them - through red, then green, then blue, then white, lighting exactly one at
# a time and nothing else. It is how you find out that the strip you think is female 2's
# belly really is female 2's belly, and that all four colours reach it.
# It ends by itself when it runs out of lights.

0 Every light is off. One lights up: female 1's head, red.
0.8 It goes out and the same segment lights green, then blue, then white - four fifths of a second each. Watch that one place in the room: four colours, a little over three seconds, then it goes dark for good.
2.4 The next segment takes over: female 1's belly O, the same four colours. Then belly P, then her feet.
12.8 Female 2, the same four segments in the same order. Then female 3.
25.6 Then the males, four lights each: the head ring, the ring above it, and the two appetite level strips.
25.6 Male 1's four, then male 2's four. The rings are the brightest thing in the room and the appetite strips the dimmest, so a male's turn looks uneven next to a female's - that is the hardware, not the test.
0 In total: twenty segments, four colours each, four fifths of a second apiece - a minute and four seconds from start to finish, if nothing is missing.
0 The page names the one that is lit right now and counts down what is left, so a segment that does not light can be identified without counting along in your head.
0 The last one goes out and the test stops itself. Every light is off again, which is also what pressing stop early does.
0 What to look for: a segment that stays dark is either an unplugged strip or a wrong pin in the firmware; a segment that lights in the wrong place is a wiring order to correct; a colour that comes out wrong on one strip and right on the others is that strip's own channel order.
